- `$&`
- `$'`  (dollar + single quote)
- `` $` `` (dollar + backtick)
- `USD $'000`

The file before G was fine (541 lines, parses). After G: 1436 lines. 1436 - 541 = 895. The corruption inserts: a fragment of my comment + a full duplicate of the file. 

Hypothesis: the edit tool implements replacement with some string processing where `$'` or `` $` `` or `$&` in the REPLACEMENT string gets special treatment — EXACTLY like JavaScript's String.prototype.replace replacement-pattern expansion!! `$&` → matched substring, `$'` → portion after match, `` $` `` → portion before match. My comment contains `$&`, `$'`, AND `` $` `` — the full set! 

Check against the observed corruption:
- Old string (the match): `    text = text.replaceAll(placeholder, xmlEscape(value));`
- New string with expansions applied:
  - `$&` → the matched old string (the replaceAll line) — would inject the old line into the output.
  - `` $` `` → everything BEFORE the match (the whole file up to the match) — would duplicate the file prefix!
  - `$'` → everything AFTER the match (the rest of the file) — duplicates the file suffix!

That precisely explains: file = prefix + [my comment text with $-patterns expanded: `$&` → old line, `` $` `` → prefix copy, `$'` → suffix copy] + suffix. The result: three `await chosen()` (original + one in the `` $` `` prefix copy + one in the `$'` suffix copy), the stray fragment ` the XML after it, and ` (text between `` $` `` and `$'` in my comment after expansion ate part of my comment), 1436 lines. And earlier — the FIRST corruption (when I batched E+F+G) — same cause: G's comment. E and F were fine both times (F's text has no $-patterns; E's neither). The md edits: A's replacement has no `$`; B contains `${ATOMATON_MACHINERY_ROOT:-.}` — contains `$` but not followed by &/'/` — `${` is not a special pattern in JS replace (only $&, $', $`, $$, $n) — safe. C/D no $.

